---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/okinawa/">Okinawa</a><span>October 4, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Okinawa &middot; October 4, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-night">The night of October 3</a></li>
          <li><a href="#before">How it came to this</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">What each side says</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">Japanese police arrested a U.S. Marine before dawn on October 4 on suspicion of strangling Anna Yagi, 39, in a hotel room in Naha, the capital of Okinawa, and stealing her wallet and backpack. The Marine, Lance Cpl. Devin Jacob Ballard, 20, denies it.<Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="沖縄署に任意同行を求め、４日未明に緊急逮捕した。" /><Cite s="ap-koja-takaichi-2026-10-07" passage="was arrested over the weekend on suspicion of strangling Anna Yagi, 39, at a hotel and stealing her wallet and backpack" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-arrest-detail-2026-10-04" passage="調べに「私は知りません」と容疑を否認しているという。" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">He is in Japanese custody because Japanese police caught him off base; under the status of forces agreement that governs U.S. forces in Japan, a suspect the U.S. military catches first stays with the U.S. until Japan charges him. Police said no handover question arose, and the U.S. ambassador, George Glass, said Ballard's custody showed that "the U.S.-Japan Status of Forces Agreement (SOFA) is working."<Cite s="okinawatimes-arrest-detail-2026-10-04" passage="県警による基地外での逮捕のため、日米地位協定を巡る身柄引き渡しの問題は生じていないという。" /><Cite s="sofa-article-17" passage="shall, if he is in the hands of the United States, remain with the United States until he is charged by Japan." /><Cite s="glass-x-2026-10-06" passage="the U.S.-Japan Status of Forces Agreement (SOFA) is working." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">On October 4 Foreign Minister Toshimitsu Motegi summoned the U.S. ambassador and Defense Minister Shinjiro Koizumi summoned the commander of U.S. forces in Japan to protest. The U.S. military offered condolences and full cooperation. It ordered every service member on Okinawa into a {figures.pauseHours}-hour pause, from noon on October 7 to noon on October 9, with no off-base liberty and no alcohol, followed by a {figures.curfewDays}-day midnight curfew. Its commander on Okinawa, Lt. Gen. Benjamin Watson, did not apologize.<Cite s="kyodo-en-2026-10-05" passage="Tokyo has lodged protests with the United States at various levels amid outrage over the case." /><Cite s="stripes-koja-protest-2026-10-05" passage="Defense Minister Shinjiro Koizumi separately summoned the commander of U.S. Forces Japan, Air Force Lt. Gen. Stephen Jost" /><Cite s="mofa-motegi-protest-2026-10-04" passage="茂木敏充外務大臣は、ジョージ・グラス駐日米国大使を外務省に召致し、抗議を行いました。" /><Cite s="watson-memo-2026-10-06" passage="From 1200 on Wednesday 7 October through 1200 on Friday 9 October 2026" /><Cite s="watson-memo-2026-10-06" passage="we will not allow off-base liberty, including a complete prohibition of alcohol consumption on or off base." /><Cite s="kyodo-en-2026-10-05" passage="when asked by reporters why he had not apologized, he said he was" /> Okinawa's new governor, Genta Koja, was elected in September with the backing of Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi's Liberal Democratic Party. He says U.S. discipline and human-rights education have not worked, and he wants the agreement reviewed.<Cite s="kyodo-en-2026-10-05" passage="Koja took office last week after winning the gubernatorial race with the backing of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party." /><Cite s="ap-koja-takaichi-2026-10-07" passage="past disciplinary measures and human rights education implemented by the U.S. military have not worked at all" /><Cite s="stripes-koja-protest-2026-10-05" passage="Koja and Chinen handed Watson and Ou a formal protest letter calling for a review of the U.S.-Japan status of forces agreement" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-night">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The night of October 3</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Anna Yagi lived in the Kokuba neighborhood of Naha.<Cite s="mainichi-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="那覇市国場の屋宜杏奈（やぎあんな）さん（39）を何らかの方法で殺害し" /> In the early hours of Saturday, October 3, she and a man appear to have taken a taxi to Naha from an entertainment district in Okinawa City, about 25 kilometers away, according to people involved in the investigation. At about 2:10 a.m. the two entered a hotel in Naha's Wakasa district.<Cite s="ryukyushimpo-referral-2026-10-05" passage="被害者とともに沖縄市から那覇市にタクシーで移動したとみられる。" /><Cite s="ap-leaders-2026-10-05" passage="in an entertainment district in Okinawa City, about 25 kilometers (15 miles) northeast of Naha" /><Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="女性は午前２時１０分ごろ、男と２人でホテルに入った。" /> Police say a hotel security camera later showed the man leaving through the entrance carrying her belongings. Footage obtained by the Ryukyu Shimpo newspaper shows a man it believes is the same one walking alone about 100 meters from the hotel before 3 a.m.<Cite s="okinawatimes-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="県警によると、防犯カメラには、容疑者が女性の所持品を手に持ってホテルの出入り口から立ち去る姿が映っていた。" /><Cite s="ryukyushimpo-footage-2026-10-06" passage="琉球新報が5日までに入手した現場付近の防犯カメラ映像に、事件当日の3日午前3時前、容疑者とみられる人物が1人で歩いている様子が記録されていた。" /><Cite s="ryukyushimpo-footage-2026-10-06" passage="入手映像は現場ホテルから100メートルほどの位置のカメラで撮影されたもので" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">At about 4:15 a.m. a hotel employee checked the room and found her body. An autopsy found she had been strangled.<Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="従業員が午前４時１５分ごろ室内を確認したところ遺体を見つけた。" /><Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="司法解剖の結果、死因は首を絞められたことによる窒息と判明した。" /> Okinawa police treated the case as a robbery and murder and put {figures.officers} officers on it. They studied the security-camera images of the man and sent plainclothes officers into the bar districts of Naha and Okinawa City.<Cite s="okinawatimes-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="島尻哲也刑事部長をトップとする１９７人体制の特別捜査本部を那覇署に設置した" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-gate-street-2026-10-04" passage="外国人らが集まる歓楽街を中心に那覇市や沖縄市などに私服の捜査員を配置した。" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">At about 11:30 that night a plainclothes officer was on Gate 2 Street in Okinawa City, a strip of bars crowded with service members on a weekend. The officer spotted a man with a shaved head and distinctive tattoos on both arms, matching the man who had gone into the hotel room with Yagi and then disappeared, though he had changed clothes. Officers waited until he was alone. He agreed to go with them to the Okinawa police station, where they arrested him before dawn on suspicion of killing her in the hotel room and taking her belongings.<Cite s="okinawatimes-gate-street-2026-10-04" passage="週末の３日午後１１時半ごろ、米兵らでにぎわう沖縄市のゲート通り。" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-gate-street-2026-10-04" passage="頭をそり上げ、両腕に特徴のあるタトゥーの入った容疑者を私服捜査員が見つけた。" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-gate-street-2026-10-04" passage="同日未明に那覇市のホテルで殺害された女性と一緒に入室して行方をくらました男の特徴と一致。" /><Cite s="yomiuri-referral-2026-10-05" passage="３日深夜に捜査員がバラード容疑者を沖縄市内で発見した際には違う服装だったが" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-gate-street-2026-10-04" passage="１人になるのを待ち、沖縄署に任意同行して緊急逮捕した。" /><Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="沖縄署に任意同行を求め、４日未明に緊急逮捕した。" /><Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="逮捕容疑は３日午前２時１０分～同４時１５分ごろ、那覇市若狭のホテル客室で" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Ballard enlisted in August 2024 and is a combat engineer at Marine Corps Air Station Futenma, a Marine air base on Okinawa.<Cite s="stripes-curfew-2026-10-06" passage="He enlisted on Aug. 8, 2024, and completed recruit training at Parris Island, S.C., according to a copy of his service record provided Tuesday by III MEF spokesman 1st Lt. Tyler Thomas." /><Cite s="stripes-curfew-2026-10-06" passage="Ballard, a combat engineer assigned to Marine Wing Support Squadron 172, Marine Aircraft Group 36, 1st Marine Aircraft Wing, is being held by Japanese authorities." /> Police say Yagi's wallet and backpack were found near the hotel on October 3. On October 5 they sent the case to prosecutors in Naha.<Cite s="yomiuri-referral-2026-10-05" passage="県警によると、財布とリュックサックは３日にホテル周辺で見つかった。" /><Cite s="stripes-koja-protest-2026-10-05" passage="The case was forwarded to the Naha Public Prosecutors Office on Monday, he said." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On October 4 her brother released a statement through the police, addressed to the press. The family was grieving, he wrote, and their parents were worn out by the loss of their daughter. He asked reporters to leave the family alone.<Cite s="okinawatimes-family-statement-2026-10-04" passage="亡くなった女性（39）=同市=の兄が4日午後、県警を通じてコメントを公表した。" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-family-statement-2026-10-04" passage="特に両親は娘を亡くし、憔悴しております。" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-family-statement-2026-10-04" passage="どうか、私たち家族をそっとしておいてください。" /> By October 6 many posts on X and other sites were blaming her for what happened to her. Koja asked people to stop, saying that when a person has been killed there is no question of fault.<Cite s="mainichi-victim-blaming-2026-10-06" passage="女性の行動に落ち度があり、事件に巻き込まれたと責める内容の投稿が多数見られる。" /><Cite s="mainichi-victim-blaming-2026-10-06" passage="人が殺されたということに対して、落ち度も何もあったものではない。慎んでいただきたい" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="rbc-phone-cord-2026-10-06" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Investigators found a cord-like mark on her neck and are examining whether the room's telephone cord was used.<Cite s="jiji-referral-2026-10-05" passage="ひも状のもので絞められた跡があり" /><Cite s="rbc-phone-cord-2026-10-06" passage="捜査関係者によると男が電話のコードで首を絞めた可能性を含めて捜査しています。" /> Police have not said how the two knew each other.<Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="県警は２人の関係など詳しい経緯を調べる。" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="before">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">How it came to this</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">When Koja met Takaichi in Tokyo on October 7, he put Okinawa's complaint this way.<Cite s="ap-koja-takaichi-2026-10-07" passage="Koja told Takaichi in a guest room at the Prime Minister's Office packed with reporters." /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Genta Koja, governor of Okinawa" when="October 7, 2026" where="Meeting with Prime Minister Takaichi" label="Associated Press">
          <p>"Okinawa has requested preventive measures every time similar crimes were repeated in past, and now we have this case again."<Cite s="ap-koja-takaichi-2026-10-07" passage="Okinawa has requested preventive measures every time similar crimes were repeated in past, and now we have this case again" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The United States governed Okinawa from the end of World War II until 1972, and its forces stayed. Of all the land in Japan set aside for the exclusive use of U.S. forces, {figures.landShare} percent is on Okinawa.<Cite s="reuters-2026-10-04" passage="which the US held from the end of World War Two until 1972" /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-base-stats-2025-07" passage="全国に占める本県の比率： 40.8% 70.3%" /> The legal status of U.S. forces is set by the agreement, signed in 1960. It gives Japan the first right to try U.S. service members for off-duty crimes, unless the only victims are other U.S. personnel, their families or U.S. property.<Cite s="sofa-article-17" passage="offenses solely against the property or security of the United States, or offenses solely against the person or property of another member of the United States armed forces or the civilian component or of a dependent" /><Cite s="sofa-article-17" passage="In the case of any other offense the authorities of Japan shall have the primary right to exercise jurisdiction." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In 1995 three U.S. servicemen raped a 12-year-old Okinawan girl, and mass protests followed.<Cite s="reuters-2026-10-04" passage="The 1995 rape of a 12-year-old Okinawan girl by three US servicemen sparked mass protests." /> The two governments then agreed that the U.S. would give "sympathetic consideration" to Japanese requests to hand over suspects before charges in murder and rape cases. Japan has made {figures.custodyRequests} such requests since, and {figures.custodyGranted} were granted.<Cite s="jc-criminal-procedures-1995" passage="The United States will give sympathetic consideration to any request for the transfer of custody prior to the indictment of the accused which may be made by Japan in specific cases of heinous crimes of murder or rape." /><Cite s="mofa-sofa-criminal-explainer" passage="6件の事件について、1995年の日米合同委員会合意に基づく起訴前の身柄引渡しの要請が行われ、そのうち、5件について起訴前の身柄引渡が実現しています" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In October 2012, after an alleged assault on a woman on Okinawa, the U.S. commander in Japan apologized personally in a statement and put every service member in the country under a curfew.<Cite s="usfj-curfew-2012-10-19" passage="I want to personally apologize for the grief and trauma the victim has endured and the anger it has caused among people in Okinawa." /><Cite s="usfj-curfew-2012-10-19" passage="I am immediately issuing a curfew to all military personnel in Japan" /> In 2016 Kenneth Franklin Gadson, a former Marine then working with the U.S. forces as a civilian, killed Rina Shimabukuro, an Okinawa resident, and the U.S. military imposed a monthlong midnight curfew and an off-base alcohol ban on Okinawa.<Cite s="stripes-koja-protest-2026-10-05" passage="Former Marine Kenneth Franklin Gadson was arrested in 2016 and later convicted of killing Okinawa resident Rina Shimabukuro." /><Cite s="stripes-koja-protest-2026-10-05" passage="Following Gadson’s arrest in 2016, the U.S. military imposed a monthlong midnight curfew and an off-base alcohol ban on U.S. military-connected personnel on Okinawa." /> Because Gadson was a civilian, the two governments then signed a supplementary pact in January 2017 on which civilians the agreement covers.<Cite s="mofa-bluebook-2017-civilian" passage="In response to the suspected murder by a civilian component of the U.S. Forces in Japan in April, both the Japanese and U.S. Governments engaged in discussions to establish effective measures" /><Cite s="mofa-civilian-agreement-signing-2017" passage="On January 16, commencing at around 3 p.m. for approximately 15 minutes, a signing ceremony took place at the Iikura Guest House" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In 2024 Okinawans learned of sexual-assault cases by U.S. service members that neither the national government nor the police had reported to the prefecture. In July 2024 Tokyo started a new system for sharing information about such cases.<Cite s="jiji-usmil-crime-2026-10-04" passage="政府や県警による県への情報提供はなく、県民にも知らされていなかったことから抗議の声が広がった。" /><Cite s="mofa-kamikawa-presser-2024-07-23" passage="これは、7月5日に、林官房長官が発表いたしました、米軍人等による性犯罪事案に係る国内情報共有体制の新たな運用" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The U.S. added military police patrols and, from October 1, 2024, barred every service member in Japan from off-base bars between 1 and 5 a.m. Joint U.S.-Japanese patrols began on Gate 2 Street in April 2025.<Cite s="usfj-rupp-message-2024-07-22" passage="This includes increased U.S. police patrols with the aim of initiating dual patrols with Japanese police in Okinawa." /><Cite s="stripes-liberty-2024-09-30" passage="Under the new regulations, no U.S. service member in Japan is permitted inside an off-base bar or “drinking establishment” between the hours of 1 a.m. and 5 a.m." /><Cite s="stripes-solo-patrols-paused-2026-03-04" passage="Joint patrols were introduced in Okinawa city on April 19 to reduce crime and misconduct involving U.S. service members in Okinawa’s entertainment districts" /> In 2025 Okinawa police cleared {figures.cases2025} criminal cases involving U.S. service members, civilian employees and their families, up from {figures.cases2024} the year before and the most since 2003.<Cite s="okinawa-police-usmil-crime-2025" passage="刑 法 犯 総 数 101 80 81 70" /><Cite s="okinawa-police-usmil-crime-2024" passage="刑 法 犯 総 数 73 80 60 68" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-crime-2025" passage="１１２件だった０３年以来２２年ぶりに１００件を超えたことが１５日、県警のまとめで分かった。" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Okinawa's government says such fixes have reached their limit. It has pressed both governments at every opportunity for a fundamental revision of the agreement, and in 2003 it began a campaign across Japan to build support. Tokyo's policy, the prefecture says, has been to respond quickly by improving how the agreement is applied.<Cite s="okinawa-pref-sofa-position" passage="県は、運用改善では限界があり、日米地位協定の抜本的見直しが必要であるとの認識に立ち、これまで機会あるごとに日米両政府に抜本的見直しを訴えてきました。" /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-sofa-position" passage="1.行動プランについて（平成15年度～）" /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-sofa-position" passage="政府は、「運用改善による機敏な対応」とする方針であり" /> That demand now comes from a governor who was elected with the backing of Takaichi's party and accepts Tokyo's plan to keep the Futenma base on Okinawa but move it to a less populated area.<Cite s="kyodo-en-2026-10-05" passage="with the backing of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party" /><Cite s="cbs-2026-10-05" passage="Koja, 42, accepts a central government plan to keep the U.S. Marine Corps' Futenma air base on Okinawa's main island, but to move it to a less populated location." /> Koja won 59 percent of the vote on September 13, 2026, and took office on September 30, four days before the arrest.<Cite s="aljazeera-koja-election-2026-09-14" passage="He won 59 percent of the vote in Sunday’s election, officials announced on Monday." /><Cite s="diplomat-2026-10-06" passage="For Governor Koja Genta, who took office on September 30, the timing was especially difficult." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="okinawa-pref-sofa-position" kind="record" detail>
          <figure class="story-figure">
            <figcaption><strong>From U.S. rule to this case</strong></figcaption>
            <DataTable columns={["When", "What happened"]} rows={chronology.rows} ariaLabel="Dated steps from U.S. rule over Okinawa to the October 2026 case" />
          </figure>
          <p>Japan's National Governors' Association resolved on July 17, 2003, to seek a fundamental revision of the agreement.<Cite s="okinawa-pref-sofa-position" passage="（平成15年7月17日日米地位協定の抜本的見直し決議）" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="stripes-liberty-2024-09-30" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The 2024 order also requires service members 19 and younger to be on base, at their off-base home or lodging, or on their way to one from 1 to 5 a.m. Ballard is 20.<Cite s="stripes-liberty-2024-09-30" passage="Additionally, all military personnel 19 years and younger" /><Cite s="stripes-liberty-2024-09-30" passage="They must be on a U.S. military installation or in an off-base home or lodging. They may also be in transit to an appropriate destination." /> The joint patrols are periodic: by May 26, 2026, the Defense Minister said, they had run 11 times.<Cite s="mod-koizumi-presser-2026-05-26" passage="このパトロールはこれまで１１回実施されてきているところ" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="stripes-el-patrols-2025-12-11" kind="record" detail>
          <p>U.S. military police began patrolling Okinawa City on their own on September 13, 2025, and Naha on November 1. The U.S. commander in Japan halted those patrols in November after military police slammed a U.S. civilian to the pavement on Gate 2 Street.<Cite s="stripes-solo-patrols-paused-2026-03-04" passage="Solo patrols in the city began Sept. 13, and expanded to Okinawa’s capital, Naha, on Nov. 1." /><Cite s="stripes-el-patrols-2025-12-11" passage="USFJ commander Air Force Lt. Gen. Stephen Jost halted unilateral patrols by the U.S. military on Okinawa and ordered an investigation into the incident, Severns said by email Nov. 26." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What each side says</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Okinawa</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Koja read his protest aloud to the top U.S. commander on Okinawa, Lt. Gen. Benjamin Watson, on October 5. "The fact that such an atrocious crime has again taken place leads us to conclude that U.S. military efforts regarding discipline and human rights education are completely ineffective."<Cite s="stripes-koja-protest-2026-10-05" passage="The fact that such an atrocious crime has again taken place leads us to conclude that U.S. military efforts regarding discipline and human rights education are completely ineffective" /> His letter asks both governments to review the agreement.<Cite s="okinawatimes-koja-kiya-2026-10-05" passage="日米両政府の責任で、日米地位協定の見直しを含め、実効性ある抜本的対策を講じるよう強く求める" /> He wants an apology to the family and to Okinawans if Ballard's guilt is established.<Cite s="koja-x-2026-10-05" passage="そのうえで罪が明らかになった場合には、ご遺族と県民に対し、しっかりと謝罪をしていただきたいと考えています。" /> He has also asked Tokyo to take part in the training U.S. forces give their personnel, rather than leaving it to the U.S. alone.<Cite s="jiji-en-koja-takaichi-2026-10-07" passage="He proposed that the Japanese government become actively involved in education rather than" /> A draft resolution in Okinawa's assembly goes further and asks the U.S. military to discipline the commanders responsible for personnel who commit serious crimes.<Cite s="ryukyushimpo-assembly-2026-10-07" passage="米軍関係者による凶悪犯罪などに対し管理責任者の処分など、米軍に組織としての責任の明確化を求める" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The United States</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Glass said the allegations do not represent the Marine Corps' values and that the system worked: Ballard is in the custody of the Okinawa police, "facing justice through the Japanese judicial system."<Cite s="glass-x-2026-10-05" passage="The criminal allegations in Okinawa do not represent the @USMC's core values of honor, courage, and commitment." /><Cite s="glass-x-2026-10-06" passage="with the U.S. servicemember in Okinawa Prefectural Police custody and facing justice through the Japanese judicial system" /> Watson offered "heartfelt sympathies" to Yagi's family. Asked why he had not apologized, he said he was "not going to comment on an ongoing investigation."<Cite s="kyodo-en-2026-10-05" passage="for the victim's family and friends, but when asked by reporters why he had not apologized, he said he was" /> "Incidents like this demand we pause for reflection," Watson wrote in his order for the pause and curfew, adding that every interaction with Okinawans "either builds or undermines the trust that is essential to our team."<Cite s="watson-memo-2026-10-06" passage="Incidents like this demand we pause for reflection." /><Cite s="watson-memo-2026-10-06" passage="Every interaction with our neighbors either builds or undermines the trust that is essential to our team." /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Tokyo</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Takaichi called the killing "extremely brutal and heinous." Her government protested and demanded full cooperation, discipline and prevention.<Cite s="stripes-koja-protest-2026-10-05" passage="Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi called the incident “extremely brutal and heinous” in a post on X that day" /><Cite s="takaichi-x-2026-10-04" passage="日本政府として米側に強く抗議するとともに、捜査への最大限の協力、米軍関係者の綱紀粛正と事件・事故の再発防止の徹底を強く求めたところです" /> Asked about revising the agreement in 2025, Motegi said "the Government has addressed specific issues one by one through the most appropriate measures that enable effective and quick responses," citing the 2017 agreement on civilians.<Cite s="mofa-motegi-presser-2025-10-22" passage="the Government has addressed specific issues one by one through the most appropriate measures that enable effective and quick responses according to the nature of the matters and cases that need to be addressed." /><Cite s="mofa-motegi-presser-2025-10-22" passage="For example, the Government concluded two supplementary agreements, one in 2015 on environmental stewardship and one in 2017 on the civilian component." /> On October 6 he said Tokyo keeps the agreement under constant review. Takaichi told Koja on October 7 that Japan and the U.S. would discuss "how to implement preventive measures that are effective."<Cite s="sanspo-2026-10-06" passage="日米地位協定改定に関しては「協定の在り方を不断に検討していきたい」と述べるにとどめた。" /><Cite s="ap-koja-takaichi-2026-10-07" passage="Japan and the U.S. will discuss “how to implement preventive measures that are effective,” she said." /> Inside her own Liberal Democratic Party, its security policy chief, Yasukazu Hamada, asked whether every case should end with nothing more than a protest.<Cite s="tbs-news23-2026-10-06" passage="毎回毎回抗議だけで終わっていいのか。前に進めるための議論をしっかりしていかなければならない" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Where he was arrested</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The Associated Press's first story, and NPR's copy of it, said an officer arrested him "after spotting him walking alone on a street in Naha’s entertainment district late Saturday."<Cite s="ap-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="An officer arrested the suspect after spotting him walking alone on a street in Naha’s entertainment district late Saturday, Kyodo said." /><Cite s="npr-2026-10-04" passage="An officer arrested the suspect after spotting him walking alone on a street in Naha's entertainment district late Saturday, Kyodo said." /> Police say he was found in Okinawa City, about 25 kilometers from the hotel, and arrested at the Okinawa police station before dawn on Sunday. The version of the AP story that PBS carried was corrected to Okinawa City.<Cite s="okinawatimes-arrest-detail-2026-10-04" passage="沖縄市の歓楽街で一人で歩いているのを捜査員が発見。" /><Cite s="jiji-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="沖縄署に任意同行を求め、４日未明に緊急逮捕した。" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-2026-10-05" passage="An officer arrested the suspect after spotting him walking alone on a street in Okinawa City's entertainment district late Saturday, Kyodo said." /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>What the crime figures count</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">AP's first story said arrests involving U.S. service members and their families "exceeded 100 last year." The police count is {figures.cases2025} cases, involving {figures.people2025} people.<Cite s="ap-arrest-2026-10-04" passage="The number of arrests related to U.S. service members and their families exceeded 100 last year, according to prefectural police." /><Cite s="okinawa-police-usmil-crime-2025" passage="刑 法 犯 総 数 101 80 81 70" /> The BBC reported "6,409 arrests" since 1972. The {figures.casesSince1972} are cases in Okinawa, involving {figures.peopleSince1972} people, among them civilian employees and family members as well as service members.<Cite s="bbc-protests-2026-10-05" passage="Since then, there have been 6,409 arrests for criminal offenses related to US military personnel, according to Okinawa Prefectural Government." /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-base-stats-2026-04" passage="計 600 1,144 3,097 274 90 1,204 6,409 413,396 1.6" /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-base-stats-2026-04" passage="計 772 1,352 2,661 230 97 1,173 6,285 176,805 3.6" /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-base-stats-2026-04" passage="３．米軍構成員等とは、米軍人、軍属、家族である。" /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <DataTable columns={["What coverage said", "Outlet", "What the police and prefecture count"]} rows={counts.rows} ariaLabel="Crime figures in coverage and what the police tables count" />
                <p class="chart-source">From the Okinawa police tables for 2025 and the prefecture's 1972 to 2025 compilation.<Cite s="cbs-2026-10-04" passage="The number of arrests related to U.S. service members and their families exceeded 100 last year, according to prefectural police." /><Cite s="aljazeera-2026-10-04" passage="Since the handover, about 6,000 US military personnel have been arrested in Japan, according to the local newspaper Yomiuri Shimbun." /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-base-stats-2026-04" passage="計 772 1,352 2,661 230 97 1,173 6,285 176,805 3.6" /></p>
              </figure>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Whether he has been charged</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Al Jazeera wrote that he "has denied the charges," and a CBS headline on October 7 said he had been "charged with murder." He has been arrested and sent to prosecutors, who have not yet decided whether to charge him. CBS's own story says he was arrested on suspicion.<Cite s="aljazeera-2026-10-04" passage="The 20-year-old marine has denied the charges." /><Cite s="cbs-curfew-2026-10-07" passage="U.S. troops on Okinawa facing curfew, alcohol ban after Marine charged with murder" /><Cite s="ap-leaders-2026-10-05" passage="Japanese prosecutors started examining the suspect’s case on Monday to determine whether to press formal charges in coming weeks." /><Cite s="cbs-curfew-2026-10-07" passage="was arrested on suspicion of killing 39-year-old Anna Yagi" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Why Japan holds him</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The BBC said Japan holds him "because the alleged crime was unrelated to official military duties." Because he was off duty and the victim was Japanese, Japan has the first right to try him. Japan holds him because its police arrested him off base. Had the U.S. military held him first, the agreement would have let it keep him until Japan charged him. Japan has asked for an early handover {figures.custodyRequests} times, and the U.S. agreed {figures.custodyGranted} times.<Cite s="bbc-protests-2026-10-05" passage="because the alleged crime was unrelated to official military duties" /><Cite s="okinawatimes-arrest-detail-2026-10-04" passage="県警による基地外での逮捕のため、日米地位協定を巡る身柄引き渡しの問題は生じていないという。" /><Cite s="sofa-article-17" passage="offenses solely against the property or security of the United States, or offenses solely against the person or property of another member of the United States armed forces or the civilian component or of a dependent" /><Cite s="sofa-article-17" passage="In the case of any other offense the authorities of Japan shall have the primary right to exercise jurisdiction." /><Cite s="sofa-article-17" passage="shall, if he is in the hands of the United States, remain with the United States until he is charged by Japan." /><Cite s="mofa-sofa-criminal-explainer" passage="そのうち、5件について起訴前の身柄引渡が実現しています" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>What the 70 percent measures</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">A later AP story said Okinawa "hosts 70% of America’s military facilities" in Japan. The prefecture's figure is {figures.landShare} percent of the land used only by U.S. forces. By count, Okinawa has {figures.facilitiesOkinawa} of the {figures.facilitiesJapan} such facilities, or {figures.facilityShare} percent.<Cite s="ap-leaders-2026-10-05" passage="Today it hosts 70% of America’s military facilities" /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-base-stats-2025-07" passage="全国に占める本県の比率： 40.8% 70.3%" /><Cite s="okinawa-pref-base-stats-2025-07" passage="沖 縄 ： 31施設 184,545千㎡" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
        <SourcedBlock source="okinawatimes-family-statement-2026-10-04" kind="checked" detail>
          <p>DW and CBS quoted the brother's grief, "My parents, in particular, are utterly devastated at the loss of their daughter," but not the brother's request that reporters leave the family alone.<Cite s="dw-2026-10-04" passage="My parents, in particular, are utterly devastated at the loss of their daughter." /><Cite s="okinawatimes-family-statement-2026-10-04" passage="どうか、私たち家族をそっとしておいてください。" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="usfj-about-2026" kind="checked" detail>
          <p>Outlets put the number of U.S. troops in Japan at 50,000 to 55,000. U.S. Forces Japan's own page says "approximately 60,000."<Cite s="usfj-about-2026" passage="USFJ includes approximately 60,000 military personnel across the U.S. Army, Marine Corps, Navy, Air Force, and Space Force elements." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">Under Japanese law, a suspect can be held in custody for at most {figures.maxDaysToCharge} days from arrest before prosecutors decide whether to charge him with the crime he was arrested for.<Cite s="moj-criminal-justice-faq" passage="The maximum period of holding suspects in custody after their arrest until the decision on whether or not to indict them is 23 days in any single crime" /> If Ballard is charged and convicted, AP reports, he could face life in prison or death.<Cite s="ap-leaders-2026-10-05" passage="If convicted, he could face life in prison or the death penalty." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The U.S. pause ends at noon on October 9. The {figures.curfewDays}-day curfew starts then: service members must be on base or at their off-base homes from midnight to 5 a.m. Watson's command will also review its rules on when service members may leave base.<Cite s="watson-memo-2026-10-06" passage="From 1200 on Wednesday 7 October through 1200 on Friday 9 October 2026" /><Cite s="watson-memo-2026-10-06" passage="you must be back on base in your assigned quarters or at your off-base domicile from midnight to 0500" /><Cite s="watson-memo-2026-10-06" passage="with recommendations to be briefed to the Okinawa Area Coordinating Committee for decision." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The councils of Naha, Ginowan and Miyakojima and the town of Motobu have passed protest resolutions unanimously. Okinawa's assembly is expected to vote on its own resolution on October 9.<Cite s="ryukyushimpo-four-councils-2026-10-07" passage="全会一致決議 那覇、宜野湾、宮古島、本部" /><Cite s="asahi-assembly-2026-10-06" passage="9日の県議会本会議で採決される見込みだ。" /><Cite s="ryukyushimpo-assembly-2026-10-07" passage="管理責任者の処分" /> Takaichi told Koja she would take seriously his request to convene the Okinawa Policy Council and respond to it. The council, where the governor and cabinet ministers discuss the bases, has not met since 2013. She gave no date.<Cite s="tokyo-np-koja-koizumi-2026-10-07" passage="知事と関係閣僚が基地負担軽減や振興策を話し合う「沖縄政策協議会」の早期開催も要求した。" /><Cite s="takaichi-x-2026-10-07" passage="私からは、沖縄政策協議会の開催については、本日の御要望をしっかりと受け止めて対応していく旨を申し上げました。" /><Cite s="tvtokyo-koja-takaichi-2026-10-07" passage="２０１３年から実施が途絶える沖縄政策協議会の早期開催についても要請したのに対し、高市総理は適切に対応する考えを明らかにしました。" /></p>
      </section>

